<content>
  <section><title>Graphs of Sequences</title>
<para>Graph the sequence {18, 36, 72, 144, 288, . . .}.</para>
<para>First, make a table:</para>
<table>
  <tgroup cols="6"><thead/>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>Term</entry>
      <entry>
        1
      </entry>
      <entry>
        2
      </entry>
      <entry>
        3
      </entry>
      <entry>
        4
      </entry>
      <entry>
        5
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>Value</entry>
      <entry>
        18
      </entry>
      <entry>
        36
      </entry>
      <entry>
        72
      </entry>
      <entry>
        144
      </entry>
      <entry>
        288
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</tgroup></table>
<para><span>Now, plot the points:</span></para>
<media><image alt="Graph of the geometric sequence." src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/f80cefc97907cdc250dfe7949abf7d406fdc14ed"/></media><para>Notice that each height is double the previous height.</para>
<para>Each term is also double the previous term.</para>
<title>Try It: Graphs of Sequences</title>
<para>Graph the following sequence and explain the pattern.</para>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>1, 3, 9, 27, . . .</para>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.
    </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Here is how to determine the pattern of a sequence with a graph:</para>
  <para>First, make a table:</para>
  <table>
    <tgroup cols="5"><thead/>
    <tbody>
      <row>
        <entry>Term</entry>
        <entry>
          1
        </entry>
        <entry>
          2
        </entry>
        <entry>
          3
        </entry>
        <entry>
          4
        </entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry>Value</entry>
        <entry>
          1
        </entry>
        <entry>
          3
        </entry>
        <entry>
          9
        </entry>
        <entry>
          127
        </entry>
      </row>
    </tbody>
  </tgroup></table>
  <para>Next, plot the points:</para>
  <media><image alt="GRAPH SHOWING PLOTTED POINTS (1, 1), (2, 3), (3, 9), AND (4, 27)." src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/9e69225cf486825f1b31d8331149e987257b2600"/></media><para>Each point is 3 times the height of the previous point.</para>
  <para>Notice that in the sequence, each term is 3 times the previous term.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
</content>
